      <section id="work" aria-labelledby="work-heading">
        <h2 id="work-heading">Selected work</h2>

        <p>
          Most of my professional work is private. Cascade is the clearest
          public example of how I approach a large system.
        </p>

        <article>
          <header>
            <h3>Cascade</h3>
            <p>
              A task platform for work that needs to run in the background,
              survive failures, and remain understandable afterward.
            </p>
          </header>

          <p>
            Today, Cascade can accept a task through its TypeScript SDK, store
            the run, execute it in a worker, and show the result and logs in a
            dashboard. The same system supports retries, schedules,
            cancellation, and replay.
          </p>

          <section aria-labelledby="cascade-context">
            <h4 id="cascade-context">Why I built it</h4>
            <p>
              Background work looks simple until a process crashes, a request is
              repeated, or a deployment changes halfway through a run. I wanted
              to understand those problems by building the complete system
              myself.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="cascade-constraints">
            <h4 id="cascade-constraints">What had to be true</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>A run could not disappear when a worker stopped.</li>
              <li>Repeating a request could not create duplicate work.</li>
              <li>One organization could never read another one’s data.</li>
              <li>
                The dashboard had to show stored system state, not temporary
                browser state.
              </li>
              <li>
                Tests had to use the real SDK, API, queue, and worker path.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="cascade-role">
            <h4 id="cascade-role">My role</h4>
            <p>
              Cascade is my personal project. I set its direction, make the
              final product and engineering decisions, and take responsibility
              for what ships.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="cascade-decisions">
            <h4 id="cascade-decisions">The important choices</h4>
            <p>
              PostgreSQL is the source of truth for runs and deployments. Redis
              handles short-lived coordination such as queues, rate limits, and
              concurrency. Large payloads can move to object storage. The
              dashboard reads the same durable records used by the workers.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="cascade-execution">
            <h4 id="cascade-execution">What exists now</h4>
            <p>
              The project includes an API, TypeScript SDK, workers, a deployment
              runner, authentication, a dashboard, logs, traces, health checks,
              and automated tests.
            </p>
          </section>

